# A local may shadow a unit-level constant of another unit -- `cc` is also
# `platform.cc`, a `cckind` value. The base of a dotted read is asked by its
# SOURCE name and only when no local answers to it, so `cc.stringview` reads
# the local under either naming scheme (`--readable-names` makes the local's
# C identifier its source name, which is what made the two diverge).
main: function is {
    cc: "/nonexistent-shadow-case".string
    hit: io.exists path: cc.stringview
    if hit then print "exists" else print "missing"
    print "cc: \{cc}"
}
